/*
 * kagi_nano: the kagi attribute-bound key, living on an Arduino Nano.
 *
 * The Nano holds the key (salted PBKDF2 commitments, never the facts). A PC
 * sends queries over USB serial and gets back yes/no. On yes, the Nano also
 * returns an HMAC proof over the PC's random challenge, so the PC knows the
 * answer came from this device and isn't a replay.
 *
 * No libraries needed. Board: Arduino Nano (ATmega328P). Serial: 115200 baud.
 *
 * Commands (one per line):
 *   PING                       -> PONG kagi-nano v1
 *   INFO                       -> INFO id=<hex> iters=<n> fails=<n> tap=<0|1>
 *   SIGN <nonce> <site_id>     -> (TAP while waiting for the button) SIGNED <hmac hex>
 *                                 or TIMEOUT. Used by the unique sign-up flow: the
 *                                 central server checks the HMAC and looks the key up.
 *   AUTH <challenge> k=v;k=v   -> WORKING, then one of
 *                                   AUTHENTICATED <scope> <hmac hex>
 *                                   DENIED
 *                                   LOCKED <seconds>
 *     <challenge> is 1-64 hex chars from the PC, or "-" when typing by hand.
 *     Keys: first_name, last_name, dob (YYYY-MM-DD or MM/DD/YYYY), ssn, pin, over
 *
 * Query kinds (same rules as kagi.py, with date of birth instead of age):
 *   Level 1:  first_name + last_name, optionally dob                 -> L1
 *   Over:     over=18|21 alone (no name needed)                      -> OVER18/OVER21
 *   Level 2:  first_name + last_name + dob + ssn + pin (all needed)  -> L2
 *
 * Commitments: C = PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256(SHA256(json(parts)), salt, ITERS)
 *   full_name  ["L1","full_name",[first,last]]
 *   dob        ["L1","dob",[first,last],dob]                 (chained on the name)
 *   ssn        ["L2","ssn",[first,last],dob,pin,ssn]         (covers everything)
 *   over T     ["OVER",T]                or random decoy bytes if under T
 *
 * Why this is stronger than the PC-only version: the commitments never leave
 * the chip (there is no command to read them), so the only way to guess is
 * through this serial port, which is rate limited (see LOCKOUT). The failure
 * counter lives in EEPROM, so unplugging the board doesn't reset it. Over
 * queries take no secret input, so there is nothing to guess: they skip the
 * lockout and aren't counted.
 */

